  "info": {
    "title": "Starter API",
    "description": "## Introduction\n\nWelcome to the **API Documentation**. This API provides a set of endpoints to help you integrate its features into your application efficiently.\n\n---\n\n**SDKs Available**\n\nTo simplify integration, we offer SDKs for popular programming languages:\n\n- [Node.js SDK](#)\n\n---\n\n<br />\n\n## Authentication\n\nThe API uses **Bearer Token** for authentication. All protected requests must include an `Authorization` header with the token:\n\n**Header Example:**\n\n```json\nAuthorization: Bearer <your_token_here>\n```\n\nTo obtain a token, authenticate via the `/auth` module. Tokens are valid for a specified duration and must be refreshed periodically.\n\n<br />\n\n---\n\n<br />\n\n## Filters\n\nFilters allow you to narrow down your query results by specifying conditions. Use query parameters to apply filters.\n\n**Example Request:**\n\n```json\nGET /resource?category=books&status=ACTIVE\n```\n\nThis example fetches resources filtered by `category=books` and `status=ACTIVE`.\n\n**Filter Field**\n\nSome endpoints provide a special field called `filter`, which allows for advanced filtering using an **OR** logic. This can be useful for querying multiple conditions on specified fields. The fields available for the `filter` parameter will be defined by the specific endpoint.\n\n**Example Request with Filter Field:**\n\n```json\nGET /resource?filter=Harry Potter\n```\n\nIn this example, the API applies the `filter` parameter across a static set of fields (e.g., `title`, `description`, and `author`) to retrieve resources that match \"Harry Potter\" in any of those fields.\n\n<br />\n\n---\n\n<br />\n\n## Pagination\n\nThe API supports pagination to manage large sets of data efficiently. Use the `offset` and `limit` query parameters to specify which part of the dataset you want to retrieve.\n\n**How It Works**\n\n- **`offset`**: Defines the starting point of the data subset. The first item is at `offset=0`.\n- **`limit`**: Specifies the maximum number of items to return in the response.\n\n**Example Request:**\n\n```json\nGET /resource?offset=0&limit=10\n```\n\nThis example retrieves the first 10 items from the dataset.\n\n**Pagination Response Format:**\n\n```json\n{\n  \"values\": [...],\n  \"meta\": {\n    \"total\": 100,\n    \"offset\": 0,\n    \"limit\": 10,\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Notes:**\n\n- **`total`**: The total number of items in the dataset.\n- **`offset`**: The starting position of the current subset.\n- **`limit`**: The number of items returned in this request.\n\nThis approach ensures efficient data retrieval while giving developers fine-grained control over paginated responses.\n\n<br />\n\n---\n\n<br />\n\n## Fields\n\nThe `fields` parameter allows you to specify which fields should be included in the response. This helps reduce the payload size by excluding unnecessary data, making your requests more efficient.\n\n**How It Works**\n\nBy using the `fields` parameter, you can define a comma-separated list of fields you want to include in the response.\n\n**Example Request:**\n\n```json\nGET /resource?fields=name,email,phone\n```\n\nIn this example, the API will only include the `name`, `email`, and `phone` fields in the response.\n\n**Example Response:**\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"John Doe\",\n  \"email\": \"johndoe@example.com\",\n  \"phone\": \"+123456789\"\n}\n```\n\n**Notes:**\n\n- If the `fields` parameter is omitted, the API will return all available fields by default.\n- Specifying invalid or unsupported field names will result in those fields being ignored, but the request will still succeed.\n- Nested fields are supported (e.g., `fields=address.city`).\n\n**Benefits of Using Fields:**\n\n- **Performance:** Reduces the size of the response payload, speeding up data transfer.\n- **Clarity:** Returns only the data relevant to your application.\n\nThis feature is ideal for optimizing API responses in applications where only specific data points are needed.\n\n<br />\n\n---\n\n<br />\n\n## Error\n\nThe API uses standardized error responses to ensure consistency and make error handling easier for developers.\n\nAll errors follow a consistent structure:\n\n| **Field**           | **Description**                                                                     | **Example**               |\n| ------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n| `statusCode`        | The HTTP status code for the error.                                                 | `400`, `404`              |\n| `error`             | A short description of the error type.                                              | `Bad Request Error`       |\n| `message`           | A detailed message explaining what caused the error.                                | `Invalid input`           |\n| `issues` (optional) | An array of key-value pairs providing details about specific issues in the request. | `[{\"email\": \"Required\"}]` |\n\n**Error Response Format**\n\n```json\n{\n  \"statusCode\": 400,\n  \"error\": \"Bad Request Error\",\n  \"message\": \"Invalid input\",\n  \"issues\": [\n    {\n      \"email\": \"Required\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n",
